The Woodland Duck Farm Supplemental Environmental Project involves the redevelopment of the Woodland Duck Farm property, a 57-acre property located along the east side of the San Gabriel River (California) just north of the confluence of the San Gabriel River and San Jose Creek. The property was purchased by The Trust for Public Land (TPL) in 2001. TPL sold the property to the Watershed Conservation Authority (WCA) in 2004. WCA plans to redevelop the Duck Farm into a multi-use property for the benefit of the local community. The SEP funds may be used for the following aspects of the Duck Farm redevelopment: 
1. Phytoremediation; 
2. Construction of groundwater recharge facilities; 
3. Wetlands habitat restoration, and 
4. Treatment wetlands, provided, however, that the SEP funds may be spent on 3. and 4., above, only in the event that SEP funds cannot be fully spent on projects 1. and/or 2.

The CA/FO requires Respondent to implement a SEP through which Respondent will spend approximately $35,000 to purchase emergency response equipment and deliver it to the San Bernadino County Fire Department (SBCFD).  Respondent must complete the SEP within sixty days of the CA/FOs effective date.  Within ninety days of the CA/FOs effective date, Respondent must provide EPA with an accounting showing the amount expended and providing substantiating documentation.  These timeframes give Respondent a reasonable amount of time to place orders, deliver equipment to the SBCFD, and complete the necessary accounting and report.

The equipment to be donated (SEP Equipment) is described in Paragraph 60 of the CA/FO to include: 

(a)	four portable kits for quick assessment of gaseous threats including levels of oxygen, combustible gasses, volatile organic compounds, carbon monoxide, and chlorine;
(b) 	a host controller with a wireless modem with an integrated global positioning system that allows integration of the four remote sensors with a command center computer; 
(c)	a case for the sensors with a built-in battery charger and a radio repeater; 
(d)	a calibration kit for calibration of each type of sensor listed above; and  
(e)	thirty hazmat level B protective clothing suits, in sizes to be selected by the SBCFD, with coverall with attached hood, elastic respirator fit closure, elastic wrists, front zipper closure, storm flap over zipper, attached boots, and outer boot flaps.
